  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Understanding the Impact of Internal Migration Dynamics on Social Protection Programmes in Malawi to Enhance Social Protection Programs Accessibility and Accuracy</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">To tackle widespread poverty, the Government of Malawi (GoM) prioritises social protection to help its poorest citizens. Thus, the National Social Support Policy (NSSP, 2012) was created. The GoM launched the Malawi National Social Support Programmes (MNSSP I 2013-2016, MNSSP II 2018-2023) and is developing the upcoming National Social Protection Strategy (MNSPS) 2023-28. These initiatives guide social protection programmes like the Social Cash Transfer Programme, Public Works Programme, and School Feeding Programme, aiming to support the &quot;ultra-poor,&quot; promote economic growth, and maintain social stability. 
 
However, migrants are often inadequately covered due to non-inclusion of mobility aspects. Their needs are frequently neglected, especially during forced displacement caused by climatic shocks. Displacement can prevent previous beneficiaries from accessing SPP support as administrative files might not be transferred, and registries are not regularly updated. Climatic events also necessitate timely updates to the beneficiary registry, a difficult task because of financial constraints. 
 
Addressing internal migration in implementing SPPs is essential for the MNSPS&apos;s goal of uplifting the poorest and integrating them into economic growth. This project aims to enhance the shock responsiveness of the SPPs in Malawi by offering recommendations, contributing to poverty reduction through sustainable resilience for IDPs and at-risk populations.</narrative>
  </description>
